  1188	\subsubsection{Sub-front and sub-super-sequences}
  1189	
  1190	When using super-sequences one is often interested in extracting sub-super-sequences which enjoy further properties.
  1191	
  1192	\begin{definition}
  1193	A sub-super-sequence of a super-sequence $f:F\to E$ is a restriction $f\restr{G}:G\to E$ to some front $G$ included in $F$.
  1194	\end{definition}
  1195	
  1196	
  1197	The following important operation allows us to understand the \emph{sub-fronts} of a given front, i.e. sub-families of a front which are themselves fronts. For a family $F\subseteq \mathcal{P}(\omega)$ and some $X\in\infSub{\omega}$, we define the sub-family
  1198	\[
  1199	F|X:=\{s\in F\mid s\subseteq X\}.
  1200	\]
  1201	
  1202	\begin{proposition}
  1203	Let $F$ be a front on $X$. Then a family $F'\subseteq F$ is a front if and only if there exists $Y\in\infSub{X}$ such that $F|Y=F'$.
  1204	\end{proposition}
