  1206	The claim is obvious if $F$ is trivial so suppose $F$ is non-trivial.
  1207	\begin{itemize}
  1208	\item[$\to$] Let $F'\subseteq F$ be a front on $Y$. Since $F'$ is not trivial either, $Y=\base F'\subseteq \base F=X$. Now if $s\in F'$ then clearly $s\in F|Y$. Conversely if $s\in F|Y$ then there exists a unique $t\in F'$ with $t\segs s \cup Y/s$ and so either $s\segm t$ or $t\segm s$. Since $F$ is a front and $s,t\in F$, necessarily $s=t$ and so $s\in F'$. Therefore $F'=F|Y$.
