   781	\subsection{Regular sequences}
   782	
   783	A monotone decreasing sequence of ordinals is, by well-foundedness, eventually constant. The limit of such a sequence exists naturally, and is simply its minimum.
   784	
   785	In general the limit of a sequence $(\alpha_i)_{i\in\omega}$ of ordinals may not exist, however any sequence of ordinals admits a limit superior. Indeed, define the sequence $\beta_i=\sup_{j\geq i}\alpha_j=\bigcup_{j\geq i} \alpha_{j}$, then $(\beta_i)_{i\in\omega}$ is decreasing and hence admits a limit.
   786	
   787	
   788	We say that $(\alpha_i)_{i\in\omega}$ is \emph{regular} if the limit superior and the supremum $\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\alpha_{i}$ of $(\alpha_i)_{i\in\omega}$ coincide. This is equivalent to saying that for every $i\in \omega$ there exists $j>i$ with $\alpha_{i}\leq \alpha_{j}$. By induction one shows that this is in turn equivalent to saying that for all $i\in\omega$ the set $\{j\in\omega\mid i<j\text{ and } \alpha_{i}\leq\alpha_{j}\}$ is infinite.
   789	
   790	\begin{definition}
   791	For $n\in \omega$ and $X$ an infinite subset of $\omega$ let us denote by $X/n$ the final segment of $X$ given by $\{k\in X\mid k>n\}$. 
   792	\end{definition}
   793	
   794	
   795	We generalise the definition of regular sequences of ordinals to sequences in quasi-orders as follows.
   796	
   797	\begin{definition}
   798	Let $Q$ be a qo. A \emph{regular sequence} is a map $f:\omega\to Q$ such that for all $n\in \omega$ the set $\{k\in\omega/n\mid f(n)\leq f(k)\}$ is infinite.
   799	\end{definition}
   800	
   801	Here is a characterisation of \wqo{} in terms of regular sequences which exhibits another property of well-orders shared by \wqo{}s.
   802	\begin{proposition}\label{prop: RegSubseq}
   803	Let $Q$ be a qo. Then $Q$ is \wqo{} if and only if one of the following equivalent conditions holds:
   804	\begin{enumWqo}[Wqo]
   805	\item \label{Item:9}Every sequence in $Q$ admits a regular sub-sequence.
   806	\item \label{Item:10}For every sequence $f:\omega\to Q$ there exists $n\in\omega$ such that the restriction $f:\omega/n\to Q$ is regular.
   807	\end{enumWqo}
   808	\end{proposition}
   809	\begin{proof}
   810	\begin{descProofUp}
   811	\item [\cref{Item:6}$\to$\cref{Item:10}] For $f:\omega\to Q$ we let $f':\omega\to \Down(Q)$ be defined by $f'(n)=\dcl\{f(k)\mid n\leq k<\omega\}$. Then clearly if $m<n$ then $f'(m)\supseteq f'(n)$. The partial order $\Down(Q)$ being well-founded by \cref{Item:6}, there exists $n\in\omega$ such that for every $m>n$ we have $f'(n)=f'(m)$. This $n$ is as desired. Indeed, if $k>n$ then for every $l>k$ we have $f(k)\in f'(k)=f'(l)$ and so there exists $j\geq l$ with $f(k)\leq f(j)$. 
   812	
   813	\item [\cref{Item:10}$\to$\cref{Item:9}] Obvious.
   814	
   815	
   816	\item[\cref{Item:9}$\to$\cref{Item:2}] By contraposition, if $f:\omega\to Q$ is a bad sequence, then every sub-sequence of $f$ is bad. Clearly a bad sequence $f:\omega\to Q$ is not regular since for every $n\in\omega$ the set $\{k\in\omega/n\mid f(n)\leq f(k)\}$ is empty. Hence a bad sequence admits no regular sub-sequence. \qedhere
   817	\end{descProofUp}
   818	\end{proof}
   819	
   820	\subsection{Sequences of subsets}\label{subsec SeqOfSubsets}
   821	
   822	In this \lcnamecref{subsec SeqOfSubsets} we give a new characterisation of \wqo{}s which enjoys both a topological and an order-theoretical flavour. 
   823	
   824	So far, we have considered $\Down(Q)$ as partially ordered set for inclusion. But $\Down(Q)$ also admits a natural topology which turns it into a compact Hausdorff $0$-dimensional space. Consider $Q$ as a discrete topological space, and form the product space $2^{Q}$, whose underlying set is identified with $\mathcal{P}(Q)$. This product space, sometimes called \emph{generalised Cantor space}, admits as a basis the clopen sets of the form
   825	\[
   826	N(F,G)=\{X\subseteq Q\mid F\subseteq X \text{ and } X\cap G=\emptyset\}, 
   827	\]
   828	for finite subsets $F,G$ of $Q$. For $q\in Q$, we write $\ClUp{q}$ instead of $N(\{q\},\emptyset)$ for the clopen set $\{X\subseteq Q\mid q\in X\}$.  Note that $\ClUp{q}^{\complement}=N(\emptyset,\{q\})$.
   829	
   830	Notice that $\Down(Q)$ is an intersection of clopen sets,
   831	\[\Down(Q)=\bigcap_{p\leq q} \ClUp{q}^{\complement} \cup \ClUp{p},\]
   832	hence $\Down(Q)$ is closed in $2^{Q}$ and therefore compact.
   833	
   834	Now recall that for every sequence $(E_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ of subsets of $Q$ we have the usual relations
   835	\begin{equation}\label{eq IntLimInfLimSupUnion}
   836	\bigcap_{n\in\omega} E_{n}\subseteq \bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}\subseteq \bigcap_{i\in \omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}\subseteq \bigcup_{n\in \omega} E_{n}.
   837	\end{equation}
   838	
   839	
   840	Moreover the convergence of sequences in $2^{Q}$ can be expressed by means of a \enquote{$\liminf=\limsup$} property. 
   841	
   842	\begin{lemma}\label{fac liminf=limsup}
   843	A sequence $(E_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ converges to $E$ in $2^{Q}$ if and only if
   844	\[\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}=\bigcap_{i\in \omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}=E.\]
   845	\end{lemma}
   846	\begin{proof}
   847	Suppose that $E=\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}=\bigcap_{i\in \omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}$. We show that $E_{n}\to E$. Let $F,G$ be finite subsets of $Q$ with $E\in N(F,G)$. Since $E=\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}$ and $F$ finite, $F\subseteq E_{j}$ for all sufficiently large $j$. Since $E=\bigcap_{i\in \omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}$ and $G$ is finite, $G\cap E_{j}=\emptyset$ for all sufficiently large $j$. It follows that $E_{j}\in N(F,G)$ for all sufficiently large $j$, whence $(E_{n})_{n}$ converges to $E$.
   848	
   849	Conversely, assume that $E_{n}$ converges to some $E$ in $2^{Q}$. If $q$ belongs to $E$ -- i.e. $E\in \ClUp{q}$ -- then $q\in E_{j}$ for all sufficiently large $j$ and thus $q\in \bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}$. And if $q\notin E$, i.e. $E\notin \ClUp{q}$, then $q\notin E_{j}$ for all sufficiently large $j$ and thus $q\notin \bigcap_{i\in\omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}$. Therefore by \cref{eq IntLimInfLimSupUnion} it follows that $E=\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}E_{j}=\bigcap_{i\in \omega}\bigcup_{j\geq i}E_{j}$.
   850	\end{proof}
   851	
   852	Observe that if $(q_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ is a perfect sequence in a qo $Q$, then for every $q\in Q$ if $q \leq q_{m}$ for some $m$, then $q\leq q_{n}$ holds for all $n\geq m$. Therefore by \cref{eq IntLimInfLimSupUnion} we have
   853	\[
   854	\bigcup_{m\in\omega}\bigcap_{n\geq m} \dcl q_{n}=\bigcup_{n\in\omega}\dcl q_{n},
   855	\]
   856	whence $(\dcl q_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ converges to $\dcl \{q_{n}\mid n\in\omega\}$ in $2^{Q}$ by \cref{fac liminf=limsup}. On the contrary no bad sequence $(q_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ converges towards $\dcl\{q_{n}\mid n\in\omega\}$, since for example $q_{0}$ does not belong to $\bigcup_{i\in \omega}\bigcap_{j\geq i}\dcl q_{j}$. We have obtained the following:  
   857	
   858	\begin{lemma}\label{fac RadoPrincipWqo}
   859	Let $Q$ be a qo. 
   860	\begin{enumThm}
   861	\item $Q$ is \wqo{} if and only if for every sequence $(q_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ there exists $N\in\infSub{\omega}$ such that $(\dcl q_{n})_{n\in N}$ converges to $\dcl\{q_{n}\mid n\in N\}$ in $\Down(Q)$.
   862	\item If $Q$ is \wqo{} and $(\dcl q_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ converges to some $D$ in $\Down(Q)$, then there is some $N\in\infSub{\omega}$ such that $D=\dcl \{q_{n}\mid n\in N\}$.
   863	\end{enumThm}
   864	\end{lemma}
   865	
   866	
   867	Actually more is true, thanks to the following ingenious observation made by Richard Rado in the body of a proof in \parencite{rado1954partial}.
   868	
   869	\begin{lemma}[Rado's trick]\label{radoLem}
   870	Let $Q$ be a \wqo{} and let $(D_{n})_{n\in\omega}$ be a sequence in $\Down(Q)$. Then there exists an infinite subset $N$ of $\omega$ such that
   871	\[
   872	\bigcup_{i\in N}\bigcap_{j\in N/i}D_{j}=\bigcup_{n\in N} D_{n},
   873	\]
   874	and so the sub-sequence $(D_{j})_{j\in N}$ converges to $\bigcup_{n\in N}D_{n}$ in $\Down(Q)$.
   875	\end{lemma}\begin{proof}
   876	Towards a contradiction suppose that for all infinite $N\subseteq \omega$ we have
   877	\begin{equation}\label{eq Rado'sTrick}
   878	\bigcup_{i\in N}\bigcap_{ j\in N/i}D_{j}\subset \bigcup_{n\in N} D_{n}.
   879	\end{equation}
   880	We define an infinite descending chain $(E_i)_{i\in\omega}$ in $\Down(Q)$. But to do so we recursively define a sequence $(N_k)_{k\in\omega}$ of infinite subsets of $\omega$ and a sequence $(q_{k})_{k\in \omega}$ in $Q$ such that
   881	\begin{enumProof}
   882	\item $N_{0}=\omega$ and $N_{k}\supseteq N_{k+1}$ for all $k\in \omega$.
   883	\item $q_{k}\in \bigcup_{j\in N_{k}}D_{j}$ and $q_{k}\notin \bigcup_{j\in N_{k+1}} D_{j}$.
   884	\end{enumProof}
   885	Suppose we have defined $N_{0},\ldots,N_{k}$ and $q_{0},\ldots q_{k-1}$. By \cref{eq Rado'sTrick} we have 
   886	\[
   887	\bigcup_{n\in N_{k}}D_{n}\nsubseteq \bigcup_{i\in N_{k}}\bigcap_{j\in N_{k}/i} D_{j},\]
   888	so we can pick $n_{0}\in N_{k}$ and $q_{k}\in D_{n_{0}}$ such that $q_{k}\notin \bigcup_{i\in N_{k}}\bigcap_{j\in N_{k}/i}D_{j}$. Then for all $i$ in $N_{k}$ let $j_{i}\in N_{k}/i$ be minimal such that $q_{k}\notin D_{j_{i}}$. Setting $n_{1}=j_{n_{0}}$ and $n_{i+1}=j_{n_{i}}$, we obtain an infinite set $N_{k+1}=\{n_{1},n_{2},\ldots\}$ which satisfies 
   889	\[q_{k}\in D_{n_{0}}\subseteq \bigcup_{j\in N_{k}}D_{j} \quad \text{and}\quad q_{k}\notin \bigcup_{j\in N_{k+1}} D_{j}.\]
   890	
   891	Now we define $E_{k}=\bigcup_{j\in N_{k}}D_{j}$. The sequence $(E_k)_{k\in\omega}$ is an infinite descending chain in $\Down(Q)$, contradicting the fact that $Q$ is \wqo{}.
   892	
   893	For the second statement, observe that if $N$ is an infinite subset of $\omega$ satisfying the statement of the lemma, then by \cref{eq IntLimInfLimSupUnion} we have
   894	\[
   895	\bigcup_{i\in N}\bigcap_{j\in N/i}D_{j}= \bigcap_{i\in N}\bigcup_{j\in N/i}D_{j}= \bigcup_{n\in N} D_{n},
   896	\]
   897	and so by \cref{fac liminf=limsup} we get that $(D_{j})_{j\in N}$ converges to $\bigcup_{n\in N} D_{n}$ in $\Down(Q)$.
   898	\end{proof}
   899	
   900	Hence if $Q$ is \wqo{}, then every sequence in $\Down(Q)$ admits a sub-sequence which converges to its union. Of course the converse also holds.
