   996	
   997	
   998	From a heuristic viewpoint, a \emph{better-quasi-order} is a well quasi-order $Q$ such that $\mathcal{P}(Q)$ is \wqo{}, $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(Q))$ is \wqo{}, $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(Q)))$ is \wqo{}, so on and so forth, into the transfinite. This idea will be made precise in \cref{sec bqoPower}, but we can already see that it cannot serve as a convenient definition\footnote{The reader who remains unconvinced can try to prove that the partial order $(3,=)$ satisfies this property.}. As the above discussion suggests, a better-quasi-order is going to be a qo $Q$, with no bad sequence, with no bad sequence of sequences, no bad sequence of sequences of sequences, so on and so forth, into the transfinite. To do so we need a convenient notion of \enquote{index set} for a sequence of sequences of~\dots\ of sequences, in short a \emph{super-sequence}. We now turn to the study of this fundamental notion defined by Nash-Williams.
   999	
  1000	\subsection{Super-sequences}
  1001	
  1002	Let us first introduce some useful notation.  Given an infinite subset $X$ of $\omega$ and a natural number $k$, we denote by $[X]^{k}$ the set of subsets of $X$ of cardinality $k$, and by $\finSub{X}$ the set $\bigcup_{k\in\omega}[X]^{k}$ of finite subsets of $X$. When we write an element $s\in [X]^{k}$ as $\{n_{0},\ldots,n_{k-1}\}$ we always assume it is written in increasing order $n_{0}<n_{1}<\ldots< n_{k-1}$ for the usual order on $\omega$. The cardinality of $s\in\finSub{\omega}$ is denoted by $|s|$. We write $\infSub{X}$ for the set of infinite subsets of $X$.
  1003	
  1004	For any $X\in \infSub{ \omega}$ and any $s\in\finSub{\omega}$, we let $X/s=\{k\in X\mid \max s<k\}$ and we write $X/n$ for $X/\{n\}$, as we have already done. 
  1005	
  1006	
  1007	\subsubsection{Index sets for super-sequences}
  1008	
  1009	Intuitively super-sequences are sequences of sequences~\dots\ of sequences. In order to deal properly with this idea we need a convenient notion of index sets. Those will be families of finite sets of natural numbers called \emph{fronts}. They were defined by \textcite{nash1965welltrees}. As the presence of an ellipsis in the expression \enquote{sequences of sequences of~\dots\ of sequences} suggests, the notion of front admits an inductive definition. To formulate such a definition it is useful to identify the degenerate case of a super-sequence, the level zero of the notion of sequence of~\dots\ of sequences, namely a function $f:1\to E$ which singles out a point of a set $E$. The index set for these degenerate sequences is the family $\{\emptyset\}$ called the \emph{trivial front}. New fronts are then built up from old ones using the following operation. 
  1010	
  1011	\begin{definition}\label{def OperationSeq}
  1012	If $X\in\infSub{\omega}$ and $F(n)\subseteq \finSub{X/n}$ for every $n\in X$, we let
  1013	\[
  1014	\Seq_{n\in X} F(n)=\big\{\{n\}\cup s\mid n\in X \text{ and }s\in F(n)\big\}.
  1015	\]
  1016	\end{definition}
  1017	
  1018	
  1019	\begin{definition}[Front, inductive definition]
  1020	%A family $F$ for $X\in\infSub{\omega}$ is a \emph{front on $X$} if it belongs to the smallest set of families of finite sets of natural numbers such that
  1021	 We define a \emph{front on $X$} simultaneously for every $X\in\infSub{\omega}$ by induction using the two following clauses: 
  1022	\begin{enumDefn}
  1023	\item for all $X\in \infSub{\omega}$, the family $\{\emptyset\}$ is a front on $X$,
  1024	\item if $X\in \infSub{\omega}$ and if $F(n)$ is a front on $X/n$ for all $n\in X$, then 
  1025	\[
  1026	F=\Seq_{n\in X} F(n)
  1027	\]
  1028	is a front on $X$.
  1029	\end{enumDefn}
  1030	\end{definition}
  1031	
  1032	\begin{remark}
  1033	In the literature, fronts are sometimes called \emph{blocks} or \emph{thin blocks}. Here we follow the terminology of \textcite{todorvcevic2010introduction}.
  1034	\end{remark}
  1035	
  1036	\begin{examples}
  1037	We have already seen example of fronts. Indeed for every $X\in\infSub{\omega}$ and every $n\in\omega$ the family $[X]^{n}$ is a front on $X$, where $[X]^{0}=\{\emptyset\}$ is the trivial front. For a new example, consider for every $n\in \omega$ the front $[\omega/n]^{n}$ and build
  1038	\[
  1039	\Schreier=\Seq_{n\in \omega} \left[\omega/n\right]^{n}
  1040	=\{s\in\finSub{\omega}\mid 1+\min s=|s|\}.
  1041	\]
  1042	The front $\Schreier$ is traditionally called the \emph{Schreier barrier}.
  1043	
  1044	\begin{figure}[ht]
  1045	
  1046	%\input{pic-FigFronts.tex}
  1047	\includegraphics{TowardsBetter-figure1.pdf} \quad \includegraphics{TowardsBetter-figure2.pdf}\\
  1048	\includegraphics{TowardsBetter-figure3.pdf} \quad \includegraphics{TowardsBetter-figure4.pdf}
  1049	\caption{Pictures of fronts}\label{FigFronts}
  1050	\end{figure}
  1051	\end{examples}
  1052	
  1053	We defined fronts in order to make the following definition.
  1054	\begin{definition}
  1055	A \emph{super-sequence} in a set $E$ is a map $f:F\to E$ from a front into $E$.
  1056	\end{definition}
  1057	
  1058	
  1059	Notice that if $F$ is a non trivial front on $X$, we can recover the unique sequence $F(n)$, $n\in X$, of fronts from which it is constructed. 
  1060	\begin{definition}
  1061	For any family $F\subseteq \finSub{\omega}$ and $n\in\omega$ we define the \emph{ray} of $F$ at $n$ to be the family
  1062	\[
  1063	F_{n}=\bigl\{s\in\finSub{\omega/n}\bigm\vert \{n\}\cup s\in F\bigr\}.
  1064	\]
  1065	\end{definition}
  1066	
  1067	Then every non trivial front $F$ on $X$ is built up from its rays $F_{n}$, $n\in X$, in the sense that:
  1068	\[
  1069	F=\Seq_{n\in X}F_{n}.
  1070	\]
  1071	
  1072	Notice that, according to our definition, the trivial front $\{\emptyset\}$ is a front on $X$ for every $X\in\infSub{\omega}$. Except for this degenerate example, if a family $F\subseteq \finSub{X}$ is a front on $X$, then necessarily $X$ is equal to $\base F$, the set-theoretic union of the family $F$. For this reason we will sometimes say that $F$ is a front, without reference to any infinite subset $X$ of $\omega$. Moreover when $F$ is not trivial, we refer to the unique $X$ for which $F$ is a front on $X$, namely $\base F$, as the \emph{base} of $F$. 
  1073	
  1074	
  1075	
  1076	Importantly, the notion of a front also admits an explicit definition to which we now turn. It makes essential use of the following binary relation.
  1077	
  1078	\begin{definition}
  1079	For subsets $u,v$ of $\omega$, we write $u\segm v$ when $u$ is an \emph{initial segment} of $v$, i.e. when $u=v$ or when there exists $n\in v$ such that $u=\{k\in v\mid k<n\}$. As usual, we write $u\segs v$ for $u\segm v$ and $u\neq v$. 
  1080	\end{definition}
  1081	 
  1082	
  1083	\begin{definition}[Front, explicit definition]
  1084	A family $F\subseteq\finSub{\omega}$ is a \emph{front on} $X\in \infSub{\omega}$ if 
  1085	\begin{enumDefn}
  1086	\item either $F=\{\emptyset\}$, or $\base F=X$,\label{def Front1}
  1087	\item for all $s,t\in F$ $s\segm t$ implies $s=t$, \label{def Front2}
  1088	\item (Density) for all $X'\in\infSub{X}$ there is an $s\in F$ such that $s\segs X'$. \label{def Front3}
  1089	\end{enumDefn}
  1090	\end{definition}
  1091	
  1092	Merely for the purpose of showing that our two definitions coincide, and only until this is achieved, let us refer to a front according to the explicit definition as a \front{}. Notice that the family $\{\emptyset\}$ is a \front{}, the trivial \front{}.  Notice also that if $F$ is a non trivial \front{} then necessarily $\emptyset\notin F$.
  1093	
  1094	Our first step towards proving the equivalence of our two definitions of fronts is the following easy observation.
  1095	
  1096	\begin{lemma}\label{lemFrontRays}
  1097	Let $F$ be a non trivial \front{} on $X\in \infSub{\omega}$. Then for every $n\in X$, the ray $F_{n}$ is a \front{} on $X/n$. Moreover $F=\Seq_{n\in X}F_{n}$. 
  1098	\end{lemma}
  1099	\begin{proof}
  1100	Let $n\in X$. For every $Y\in\infSub{X/n}$ there exists $s\in F$ with $s\segs \{n\}\cup Y$. Since $F$ is non trivial, $s\neq\emptyset$ and so $n\in s$. Therefore $s'=s\setminus \{n\}\in F_{n}$ with $s'\segs Y$, and $F_{n}$ satisfies \cref{def Front3}. Now if $F_{n}$ is not trivial and $k\in X/n$, there is $s\in F_{n}$ with $s\segs \{k\}\cup X/k$ and necessarily $k\in s\subseteq \base F_{n}$. Hence $\base F_{n}=X/n$, so condition \cref{def Front1} is met. To see \cref{def Front2}, let $s,t\in F_{n}$ with $s\segm t$. Then for $s'=\{n\}\cup s$ and $t'=\{n\}\cup t$ we have $s',t'\in F$ and $s'\segm t'$, so $s'=t'$ and $s=t$, as desired. The last statement is obvious.
  1101	\end{proof}
  1102	
  1103	Our next step consists in assigning a rank to every \front{}. To do so, we first recall some classical notions about sequences and trees.
  1104	
  1105	\begin{definition}
  1106	For a non empty set $A$, we write $A^{n}$ for the set of sequences $s:n\to A$. Let $A^{<\omega}$ be the set $\bigcup_{n\in\omega} A^{n}$ of finite sequences in $A$. We write $A^{\omega}$ for the set of infinite sequences $x:\omega \to A$ in $A$. Let $u\in A^{<\omega}$, $x\in A^{<\omega}\cup A^{\omega}$.
  1107	\begin{enumDefn}
  1108	\item $|x|\in \omega+1$ denotes the length of $x$.
  1109	\item For $n\leq |x|$, $x\restr{n}$ is the initial segment, or prefix, of $x$ of length $n$.
  1110	\item  We write $u\segm x$ if there exists $n\leq |x|$ with $u=x\restr{n}$. We write $u\segs x$ if $u\segm x$ and $u\neq x$.
  1111	\item We write $u\conc x$ for the concatenation operation.
  1112	\end{enumDefn}
  1113	\end{definition}
  1114	
  1115	Identifying any finite subset of $\omega$ with its increasing enumeration with respect to the usual order on $\omega$, we view any \front{} as a subset of $\omega^{<\omega}$. 
  1116	Notice that under this identification, our previous definition of $\segm$ for subsets of $\omega$ coincides with the one for sequences. 
  1117	
  1118	\begin{definition}\label{def trees}
  1119	\begin{enumDefn}
  1120	\item A \emph{tree} $T$ on a set $A$ is a subset of $A^{<\omega}$ that is closed under prefixes, i.e. $u\segm v$ and $v\in T$ implies $u\in T$. 
  1121	\item A tree $T$ on $A$ is called \emph{well-founded} if $T$ has no infinite branch, i.e. if there is no infinite sequence $x\in A^{\omega}$ such that $x\restr{n}\in T$ holds for all $n\in\omega$. In other words, a tree $T$ is well-founded if $(T,\mges)$ is a well-founded partial order.
  1122	
  1123	\item When $T$ is a non-empty well-founded tree we can define a strictly decreasing function $\rho_{T}$ from $T$ to the ordinals by transfinite recursion on the well-founded relation $\sges$:
  1124	\[
  1125	\rho_{T}(t)=\sup\{\rho_{T}(s)+1 \mid t\segs s\in T\} \quad\text{for all $t\in T$.}
  1126	\] 
  1127	It is easily shown to be equivalent to
  1128	\[
  1129	\rho_{T}(t)=\sup\{\rho_{T}(t\conc (a))+1\mid a\in A \text{ and } t\conc(a)\in T\} \quad\text{for all $t\in T$.}
  1130	\]
  1131	The \emph{rank} of the non-empty well-founded tree $T$ is the ordinal $\rho_{T}(\emptyset)$. 
  1132	\end{enumDefn}
  1133	\end{definition}
  1134	
  1135	For any \front{} $F$, we let $T(F)$ be the smallest tree on $\omega$ containing $F$, i.e.
  1136	\[
  1137	T(F)=\{s\in\omega^{<\omega}\mid \exists t\in F \ s\segm t\}.
  1138	\]
  1139	
  1140	The following is a direct consequence of the explicit definition of a front.
  1141	\begin{lemma}\label{lem TreeFrontWellFounded}
  1142	For every \front{} $F$, the tree $T(F)$ is well-founded.
  1143	\end{lemma}
  1144	\begin{proof}
  1145	If $x$ is an infinite branch of $T(F)$, then $x$ enumerates an infinite subset $X$ of $\base F$ such that for every $u\segs X$ there exists $t\in F$ with $u\segm t$. Since $F$ is a \front{} there exists a (unique) $s\in F$ with $s\segs X$. Let $n=\min X/ s$ and for $u=s\cup \{n\}$ consider some $t\in F$ with $u\segm t$.
